        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The audience values alternative methods of political engagement beyond traditional channels.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Feeling connected to the political system increases democratic legitimacy and motivation to participate.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "People who feel disconnected believe they lack influence over political outcomes.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Music-based interventions can foster emotional resonance with political issues.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 83108,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83108.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think it is broken in a lot of respects, but it's broken, but it's redeemable. And what I tell people who get very frustrated by what they see on TV or the quality of the representation that they have, whether it's in Congress or elsewhere. As I say, the smartest thing to do is to get involved earlier. So if you just wait for your congressional seat, most people live in highly gerrymandered districts. And so you have a choice, but the outcome is almost predetermined based on the way the districts cut. So your only chance really, and this is an important chance, is to get involved at the primary level. And when these seats come open, usually there will be a flood of candidates who want to take hold. And it's oftentimes pretty easy to figure out who amongst the primary candidates is representing or positioning themselves in a way that you respected like. And so I think that's That's the best thing I would say with respect to a lot of these representative positions is, hey, look, you know, get involved early because you can really make an impact on a campaign. And then you can see the right candidate through. And then the other thing is, you know, the majority of the decisions that are going to affect your daily lives are actually going to be made in Washington. They're probably going to be made at your city council level or your state house. And, you know, again, chances are you're going to be able to figure out who your mayor is or who's on city council or who represents you at the local level and usually get old. And so, you know, in that respect. But earlier in the more involved, you get better and the more hope you'll derive from the system, I think.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
