    "turn_text": "Yeah so you know I kind of fell in my lap in a way I didn't necessarily campaign on but so what happened is because you don't know. All its sports, the athletes cannot market themselves at all. And that means you can't do traditional marketing like you think about like a shoe dealer and anything like that. But it also means you can't coach a camp, you can't teach swim lessons, you can't do anything. The college athlete is the only person in America who has that restriction as far as I know. So like I remember Congress, I don't have that restriction. I could write a book, I could. a lot of things. And so I probably shouldn't have that restriction, by the way. But in any of it, so what you would brought this to the fore is the state of California and now a handful of other states and pass their own individual laws that say if you're a college athlete in our state, you can do x, y, z. Well, all laws look different. So that means that depending on where you are, you're going to have different rules around how you can capitalize on your own neighbor's likeness. I think that's chaos, frankly, as a former athlete. I wouldn't want to exist in that system. I would want to have one standard by which we all have to operate. And so that's what this bill would do. We set a federal standard that allows for an image and likeness so it extends that economic right. But it also has what I believe are important guardrails to protect. the sanctity of college sports and to prevent bad actors from getting involved and turning it into a sort of pay-for-play rivalry type deal. So that's sort of the goal. And again, everybody thinks about the college football player and basketball player, but really don't work for all sports. My wife was a college",
