    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83108.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "So actually that last point. So there's one bill that I think goes way beyond what is appropriate. That would basically professionalize college sports. So it has revenue splits. That's a 50% of revenue has to go here, has full healthcare benefits, has some other items. Those are the big ones. And some folks are interested in that, which I suppose I understand, but what I don't think they realize is If you went down that path very quickly, and I've been told this by athletic administrators, but very quickly, all the sports will go away, except for the revenue sports. So, you know, by my field hockey, probably by my baseball, by my wrestling, by my swimming, you know, all these sports that provide an incredible platform for students to go to great school get an amazing education that they otherwise would get in competitive high level, those would go away and you just have football and basketball and then whatever you need to fill out the title line requirements basically. But what a lot of administrators have told me is We would either do that or we'd just cut sports altogether, because we don't want to be in a business where we're employing our student athletes. And the one thing that I think one of the things people don't realize is if you are employing student athletes, you're hiring student athletes. So that means you're firing student athletes too, if they don't pan out. And so I think of my own self, but also guys I played with. If you would have evaluated me 12 months into my college career, maybe we should send him back to Cleveland. He's not mature enough. He's not doing all the right stuff. And maybe we should move on from this athlete. Maybe I deserved that. But I think the system as it sets up today, it's conducive to an educational environment. It's conducive to a notion that you're going to take 16 to 18-year-olds bring them from all different backgrounds, some really tough backgrounds. of them in these great environments and given the opportunity to grow and learn and become great athletes, but also hopefully are a meaningful degree. And so, you know, anything that disrupts that. and reduces those opportunities, I think is a big negative. And that's a lot of the competing bills out there are trending. Yeah, and so true, because they're well-intentioned, but they destroy college sports.",
