    "turn_text": "little bit of both. Two things. One, my family, we immigrated here from Cuba, so my father escaped the Castro regime. He was a little boy, his parents, and brought him along. We've lived in Northeast Ohio my entire life. You've always just been so grateful of what this country means. what it can do for people and it's it literally was our hope it was our hope that allowed us a place to escape to and so we've already you know it's always been a sort of a dinner table conversation and how great the country is and Then on top of that, you know, after the 2016 election, just looking at the environment, the political environment, it just felt totally hopeless. And it was going in the direction that, frankly, just being deeply uncomfortable. And I was scared for our country, just based on the rhetoric and the way people were behaving and how unproductive our political system had become. All like we were losing so much of what I remembered growing up as a parent. And, you know, I always, my grandmother, well, she was one of my best friends. And he always said, if you feel your country, going away or moving away from you, you gotta get involved and you gotta do something because that's fine. Any advice? Yeah, and so I felt that and sort of with all that background and all those thoughts in my head, my wife and I made a tough decision but wanted to get involved.",
