  },
  {
    "episode_id": 83108,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83108.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "I think there are multiple. So the way I think about this, you have to ask yourself, who is China and what do they want? So China is a totalitarian, communist regime controlled by President Xi and various other people. And what they say goes. And that's who China is. What is their goal? What do they want? They want to displace the United States as the global and put them in the driver's seat. Now, so how would that impact our daily lives? If you look at the post-World War II order, it was essentially built by the United States and our allies based on fundamental basic human rights and liberal democratic values, small L, small D liberal democratic values, the values that underpin our Constitution. That is how the world order has evolved since World War II. And over that time, we've had the longest sustained peace, global peace, in 250 years. We've pulled more people out of poverty, more people have become democratic. The world has grown and evolved in so many positive ways as a result of our leadership and the values that we adhere to. if you take us out and you put China in a driver's seat. All of the entire global system will be based on Chinese Democratic or Chinese Communist values. And they've said that they want the world to be informed by what they call Chinese wisdom. And so that is a direct threat to who we are, how we operate, how we would do business, how we would educate our kids, what we'll be able to say or not say. It's such a total affront to the American system. that I think the threat, it's hard to sort of say it's this, that, and the other, I think it's a total threat. So what do you do in that world? And my argument would be, if you have somebody that powerful, that big, that strong, that believes that you need to be removed from the global stage and they need to be the new global superpower. I would argue that the things that you can collaborate with on that group are pretty slim. These are nothing that has anything to do with national security. That's part of the unique challenge because our economies are so intertwined. How do you do that? But I think over time, we need to start. building coalitions out, so we need strong partnerships with our allies, and we need to be unwavering in our commitment to our values. We just cannot hold on.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
